WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.625
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:03.030
OK, so if you've been
following along series,

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.410
you've heard me talk about
Cloud DLP and all the ways

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:07.080
that it can help you
inspect your data

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.410
and de-identify it with a whole
bunch of different techniques.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.360
We talked about the concepts,
and we saw some code.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.120
But if you're wondering
what DLP looks

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.250
like from a pointy,
clicky perspective,

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.087
stick around for this
extended episode,

00:00:19.087 --> 00:00:21.420
where I'll show you some DLP
functionality in the Google

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:22.003
Cloud Console.

00:00:22.003 --> 00:00:24.630
[DOOR CLOSING AND LOCKING]

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.582
[MUSICAL CHORD]

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.030
So for this demonstration,
we're going to do a few things.

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:34.958
What we have is a GCS
bucket with few files.

00:00:34.958 --> 00:00:37.250
I'm going to show you how to
create an inspect template

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:39.000
and then kick off an
inspection job that's

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.640
going to tell us
which files might

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.250
have some sensitive
information in them.

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:45.320
Then after that,
I'll show you how

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.300
to create a
de-identification template

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.850
and then use that to create
an identical data set

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:51.550
in BigQuery with
encryption applied

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.300
to some sensitive fields.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.347
OK, so before we
start, let me just

00:00:55.347 --> 00:00:57.180
show you real quick
what we're working with.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:03.200
So I have a GCS
bucket, some data.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:07.730
And in that bucket, we just
got some text files, CSV files.

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:09.380
Some of them have
sensitive values.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:10.840
Some of them don't.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.410
Like, for example,
we have sample_s10

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:24.200
has some phone numbers,
while s20 has names

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.930
and Social Security numbers.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:29.445
So it's a mixed bag.

00:01:29.445 --> 00:01:30.320
We've got full names.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:31.653
We have social security numbers.

00:01:31.653 --> 00:01:33.655
We've got phone numbers.

00:01:33.655 --> 00:01:35.030
So what we're
going to start with

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:41.230
is a DLP inspection template.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:44.520
Let's get rid of this guy.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.783
So creating a template
here is pretty simple.

00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:48.200
You go to the Data
Loss Prevention

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.290
page, Create Template.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:53.750
And here, you'll choose
either an inspect template

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:54.890
or a de-identify template.

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:58.870
We'll go for inspect for now.

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:04.690
OK, let's call our
bucket_inspect template,

00:02:04.690 --> 00:02:05.450
a bucket.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.115
All right, hit Continue.

00:02:15.115 --> 00:02:16.490
And now, here's
the part where we

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.910
choose what infotypes we
actually want to inspect for.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:23.420
So I'm just going to go for--

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:24.320
since we saw that.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:30.670
We saw Social Security numbers,
and let's add a phone number.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:33.470
Boom, added, done.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:36.320
And then here, we choose
the confidence threshold

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.530
or likelihood.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:40.940
So it can go, again, it can
go from very unlikely to very

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:41.495
likely.

00:02:41.495 --> 00:02:43.370
We'll just hit Possible,
right in the middle.

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:45.670
Hit Create.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.770
Give that a second.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.030
All right, so here is
our inspection template.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.360
And here are the two infotypes
that we're looking for,

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:54.810
the likelihood right there.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:56.270
So now that we
have that template,

00:02:56.270 --> 00:03:00.202
we can go over here, back to
the Data Loss Prevention page,

00:03:00.202 --> 00:03:05.550
and we can now create
a job or a job trigger.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:07.425
All right.

00:03:07.425 --> 00:03:09.050
So the cool thing
about DLP is that you

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:13.040
can make it inspect
on a schedule,

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.830
so we'll call this
our weekly_inspection.

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:21.890
And what are we
going to inspect?

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:24.205
It's going to be
Google Cloud Storage.

00:03:24.205 --> 00:03:25.580
And you can see
that you can also

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.860
do BigQuery or Cloud Datastore.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:37.740
All right, and we'll make our
bucket name sample_data_dlp.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.940
All right, and here, if you
scroll down a little bit,

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:47.843
you can actually
choose to use sampling.

00:03:47.843 --> 00:03:50.010
So we're not going to use
sampling for this example.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:52.050
But if you have a
very large data set,

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:55.510
you might want to use sampling,
because the more files it scans

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.360
for, the longer that
job is going to take

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.660
and the more resources
it's going to take up.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:02.790
And then when you do choose to
use sampling, you can always

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:07.530
adjust it here, whether you want
to sample 50% or 20% or 100%--

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.390
which wouldn't make
sense-- but 100%.

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:12.300
But for our purposes, we're
going to say no sampling.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:17.470
Here, you can also
choose the files

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:20.200
that you want to limit
your inspection to.

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:23.907
We'll just leave it at All
for now, and hit Continue.

00:04:23.907 --> 00:04:25.240
And now, we choose our template.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:26.300
We only have one
template, so it's

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.540
going to autopopulate our other
stuff, the templates that we

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:29.040
have.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.960
And there it is,
our bucket_inspect.

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:33.160
And here are the details.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:37.370
Again, here are two infotypes
and the likelihood of possible.

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:41.780
So for each job, aside
from just a template,

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:43.900
you can also add
some infotypes here.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:47.493
But again, the more
infotypes you look for,

00:04:47.493 --> 00:04:49.035
the longer that job
is going to take.

00:04:49.035 --> 00:04:51.610
So what I'm going to
do is just deselect

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:55.150
any additional infotypes,
so we have nothing.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:58.280
We'll leave the likelihood,
again, as possible.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:00.460
Hit Continue.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:03.250
And now, here are the actions
that you want your job

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:05.620
to take after it's done.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:08.320
So we're going click
here, save to BigQuery,

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.280
and I'll show you why that's
important in second, but--

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:14.526
All right, our
project, a-dlp-demo.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:20.380
Our data set is bucket_inspect.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:23.470
So I created these data sets
ahead of time in BigQuery.

00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:25.210
So before you do
this, you'd want

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:27.990
to do that yourself,
create your own data

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:32.300
set and then also
an empty table.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.330
Inspect.

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:35.830
And there are other
actions that you

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:39.550
can add to each job, like
Publish to Pub/Sub or Notify

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:40.810
by Email.

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:42.600
But for now, we'll
just go to a BigQuery.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.830
Hit Continue.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:48.040
And here, you can choose
when you want it to run.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:52.030
So you can run daily, weekly,
or custom, so we'll keep it

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:52.580
at weekly.

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:54.446
Hit Continue.

00:05:54.446 --> 00:05:58.630
And that's going to give us a
recap of our job configuration

00:05:58.630 --> 00:05:59.980
in the JSON objects.

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:02.660
Scroll down past that.

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:06.880
We're going to hit Create,
and we're going to Confirm.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:09.130
So now, it's creating
that trigger.

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:11.110
Since it's a weekly one,
it's not running yet.

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:13.300
So we just hit here, Run Now.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:18.310
Boom, and they're running
trigger weekly inspection.

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:20.090
So an inspection job
could take some time,

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.630
and that depends basically
on how big your data set is

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:26.430
or how many infotypes you
specified in that template.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:29.362
All right, so we must
see how that job went.

00:06:29.362 --> 00:06:31.570
You want to go back to the
Data Loss Prevention page,

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:34.210
and under Inspect
Jobs, there it is.

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:36.910
Click that, and this is
pretty much a recap page

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:38.070
of how the job went.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:40.770
So you'll see that
it found 52 findings.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:42.820
52 findings were
found in your data.

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:45.092
And 44 of them were phone
numbers, and 8 of them

00:06:45.092 --> 00:06:46.300
were Social Security numbers.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:49.030
So there's not much
more information

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:50.668
on this page than that.

00:06:50.668 --> 00:06:52.960
If you want more information
about each of the findings

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.780
that it made, you
want to see what

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:57.580
it threw out in to BigQuery.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:06.280
So these are all the fields
associated with each finding

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:09.640
that it made in your data, so
I'll show you a quick query.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:13.600
We're going to select
the file name, which

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.780
should be here, relative_path.

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:19.450
And we want to include--

00:07:19.450 --> 00:07:22.730
what else-- info_type
and likelihood.

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:26.650
Hit Run, and there you go.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:30.970
So it's not just the file
has sensitive values in it,

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:32.790
and it made an
entry in a database.

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:36.020
It actually made an entry for
each finding that it made.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:39.550
So, for example, you'll
see that sample_s08 is here

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:42.550
a few times because it
has a few phone numbers.

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:44.050
And you have the
option of including

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:46.470
the actual value of
that data, but I didn't

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:49.210
enable that for this example.

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:54.280
But you can see how much
sensitive data a file

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:56.530
might have rather than
just whether or not

00:07:56.530 --> 00:07:59.800
it does have that sensitive
information in it.

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:02.530
An inspection job is a great way
to find out if your data might

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:04.760
have any sensitive value in it.

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:07.360
Now, let's create a
de-identification template

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:09.260
and see what we
can do with that.

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:13.030
OK, so before we create a
de-identification template,

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:15.200
I just want to show you
something real quick.

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:17.560
Since we're going
to use tokenization

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:22.397
to de-identify information,
on their cryptographic keys,

00:08:22.397 --> 00:08:23.980
I'll show you how
to create a key ring

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:28.887
that I named dlp_keyring and
a key that I named dlp_key.

00:08:28.887 --> 00:08:30.470
And we're going to
use it in a second.

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:33.610
So let's jump back to
Data Loss Prevention.

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:36.440
Once again, we're going to
go up here, Create Template.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.939
This time instead of
Inspect, De-identify.

00:08:39.939 --> 00:08:44.530
We'll call this bucket_deid,
and this is our Bucket

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:48.650
deidentification template.

00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:49.626
Great.

00:08:49.626 --> 00:08:51.160
Hit Continue.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:53.910
And here, we'll pick
a transformation rule.

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:55.910
And there are some
familiar faces in here.

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:58.800
We see redaction, replacement,
replace of infotype.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:02.108
Down here are our three
tokenization methods.

00:09:02.108 --> 00:09:04.150
We're going to choose
Cryptographic Deterministic

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:05.800
Token.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:08.530
Down here, we're going to
paste in that key information,

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:12.040
the resource name as a path.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:14.030
And here, it's going
to want a wrapped key,

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:15.405
so it's pretty
much going to want

00:09:15.405 --> 00:09:17.840
a key that's been encrypted
by that encryption key.

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:20.340
And I'll show you how to
generate that real quick down

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:20.840
here.

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:24.890
I'll open up our
Cloud Shell terminal.

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:32.830
And we're going to set this
variable to openssl rand

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:37.550
-base64, 16 characters, boom.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:39.920
And then here's what
that looks like, right?

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.551
So we're going to take that.

00:09:42.551 --> 00:09:46.320
We'll take off any spaces.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:51.715
And we're going to pipe that
into gcloud kms encrypt.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:58.860
And then we're going
to give it our key ring

00:09:58.860 --> 00:09:59.910
and our key information.

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:02.970
So location was global.

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:06.750
Key ring was dlp_keyring.

00:10:10.376 --> 00:10:15.260
Our key, dlp_key.

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:18.250
And we're going to
specify plaintext-file,

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:21.370
which is actually going
to be the value that we

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:22.940
piped into this command.

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:26.820
And then we're going to specify
a ciphertext-file, which

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:32.290
is the output that we want
it to go to, so ciphertext.

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:33.210
Hit Enter.

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:38.070
And what we got now is a file
that has some cipher text.

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:41.550
So we can't paste that in here,
so let's encode it to base 64--

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:43.400
hold on, let's clear that up--

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:50.850
base64, no wrapping,
and ciphertext.

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:53.910
All right, so let's
take this guy,

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:58.830
carefully select it, put it on
a clipboard, paste it over here,

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:01.230
and boom, there it is.

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:04.650
All right, so let's
get rid of this

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:07.380
and continue on
with the template.

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:10.710
So with Surrogate InfoType,
again, this the value that's

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:16.152
going to prepend in front
of the encrypted values.

00:11:16.152 --> 00:11:18.360
So since we're only going
to look for Social Security

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:20.440
numbers, I'll just
make it SSN, and we'

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:23.560
going to specify the
infotype down here.

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:27.990
Manage InfoTypes, OK, there it
is, US Social Security number.

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:30.180
And again, you can
choose up to 150.

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:33.780
But for now, Social
Security number is fine.

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:36.085
And we'll hit Create.

00:11:36.085 --> 00:11:38.670
So the cool thing about
the deID templates,

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:41.670
you can go over here to test it
out to make sure everything's

00:11:41.670 --> 00:11:43.120
cool, everything's working.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:46.890
So a Social Security number
is three digits, two digits,

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:48.520
and four digits.

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:51.180
And that is an
example of what it's

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:52.920
going to look like
when it's transformed,

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:55.402
and there's our surrogate
value right here.

00:11:55.402 --> 00:11:56.860
So to demonstrate
de-identification

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:59.700
in action, what I'm going to
do is create a dataflow job,

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:02.460
which is going to take our
CSV files from our bucket

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:05.190
and then make a
de-identified version

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:08.670
of that same data in BigQuery.

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:11.220
So to create a dataflow
job, let's head over

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:17.820
here to the Navigation menu,
scroll down, Dataflow, Create

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:19.750
Job from Template.

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:25.040
So a job name, let's just call
it Bucket de-identification,

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:26.813
deID for short.

00:12:26.813 --> 00:12:28.230
And under Dataflow
Template, we're

00:12:28.230 --> 00:12:31.470
going to choose Data
Masking/Tokenization from Cloud

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:33.570
Storage to BigQuery
Using Cloud DLP.

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:39.050
And now, we must
specify that bucket

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:40.650
that I showed you earlier.

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:45.120
I have a sample_data_dlp,
and we want

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:48.910
to target the CSV files
that are in there.

00:12:48.910 --> 00:12:50.880
So I don't know if
you noticed before,

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:53.640
but there was a
data set in BigQuery

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:58.200
that was called bucket_deid,
totally empty, no

00:12:58.200 --> 00:12:59.010
tables or anything.

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:01.320
This will create
the tables for us.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:04.665
And our project ID, dlp_demo.

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:12.120
And the template name,
which is on my clipboard,

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:17.700
temporary location is going
to be in the same bucket slash

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:19.830
temp.

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:21.720
Great.

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:23.203
All right, let's look this over.

00:13:23.203 --> 00:13:24.870
So now what's happening
is that dataflow

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:27.450
is going through all of
those CSV files in our bucket

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:30.510
and then creating an
identical data set

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:32.810
in BigQuery with those
values de-identified

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:34.485
using tokenization.

00:13:34.485 --> 00:13:36.360
So once the dataflow is
done doing its thing,

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:38.402
here is what it's going
to look like in BigQuery.

00:13:38.402 --> 00:13:40.950
So here's our data
set, bucket_deid.

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:42.530
And now, in that
data set, there's

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:45.800
a table for each CSV file
that was in that bucket

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:47.220
that I showed you earlier.

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:48.720
So if you remember
before, we looked

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:51.420
at sample_s20 that had a bunch
of names and Social Security

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:52.320
numbers.

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:54.150
Let's query that table
and see what we get.

00:13:57.880 --> 00:14:00.000
And boom, there it is.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.400
Just like before, we've got
name, Social Security number,

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:03.240
and some other data.

00:14:03.240 --> 00:14:04.950
But now, those Social
Security numbers

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:07.182
are completely unrecognizable,
because they've

00:14:07.182 --> 00:14:08.640
been encrypted with
the key that we

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:11.320
specified in the
de-identification template.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:13.680
So now, this data
is essentially safe

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:15.690
to use for any analytic
workflows that might

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:17.640
exist in your organization.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:18.822
And that's it.

00:14:18.822 --> 00:14:20.280
That was just a
quick taste of what

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:22.460
cloud DLP is capable of doing.

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:24.960
But if you have your own stories
of how cloud DLP has helped

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:27.540
you secure your data, feel free
to share that in the comments

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:28.540
below.

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:29.663
Thanks for watching.

00:14:29.663 --> 00:14:30.330
Until next time.

